<cell>Show that the projection into the <m>xy</m>-plane of the 
curve of intersection of the parabolic cylinder 
<m>z = 1 - 5 y^2</m> and the paraboloid <m>z = x^2 + y^2</m> 
is an ellipse.
(a) Find a vector-parametric equation 
<m>\vec{r}_1(t) = \langle x(t), y(t), z(t) \rangle</m>
for the ellipse in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
Shadow: <m>\vec{r}_1(t) =</m> <fillin characters="40" name="AnSwEr0001"/>
for <m>0 \leq t \leq 2\pi</m>.
(b) Find a vector-parametric equation for the curve
of intersection of the parabolic cylinder and the
paraboloid.
Intersection: <m>\vec{r}_2(t) =</m> <fillin characters="40" name="AnSwEr0002"/>
for <m>0 \leq t \leq 2\pi</m>.</cell>
<cell>
<image pi:generated="webwork/images/Library__Hope__Multi2__09-02-Parametric-curves__Parametric-curves-3D-01__Parametric-curves-3D-01.pg-image-1.png" width="56%"/>
<p/></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\left&lt;\cos\mathopen{}\left(t\right),\frac{\sin\mathopen{}\left(t\right)}{\sqrt{6}},0\right&gt;</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\left&lt;\cos\mathopen{}\left(t\right),\frac{\sin\mathopen{}\left(t\right)}{\sqrt{6}},1-\frac{5\sin^{2}\mathopen{}\left(t\right)}{6}\right&gt;</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
